{
  "dom_id": "step-q7869",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14246252/assets/569d6855e7ebdbd7c2e040d7f0200e72e0df71f88991085e3224570587c33767.png)\n\nThe point $A$ with coordinates $(2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ and the point ${A}^{′}$ with coordinates $(6,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax),$ shown above. What is the value of $a?$",
  "worked_solution": "The graph of $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax)$ can be obtained from $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ by a horizontal stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{a}.$ \n\nFrom the diagram, we deduce that under such a transformation, the point $(2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ that lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ becomes \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{a}\\cdot 2,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=(6,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\n$$\n\nwhich lies on $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(ax).$ \n\nTherefore, $a=\\frac{1}{3}.$",
